{
  "components": [
    {
      "id": "chat_1",
      "provider": "chat",
      "name": "Chat",
      "config": {
        "hideForm": true,
        "mode": "Source",
        "parameters": {},
        "type": "chat"
      },
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 20,
          "y": 200
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "agent_rocketride_1",
      "provider": "agent_rocketride",
      "name": "RocketRide Wave",
      "config": {
        "agent_description": "Launch Kit app-profile analyst",
        "instructions": [
          "You are Launch Kit's app analyst. You are given a GitHub repository URL and a live product URL for an app published on the RocketRide App Store. Your job is to produce a complete, accurate APP PROFILE as strict JSON.",
          "PROCESS: 1) If a repository URL is given, use the GitHub tool to read it: the README, package manifests (package.json / pyproject.toml / etc.), directory structure, and any docs/ or landing copy. A repo may be ABSENT (none supplied) or UNREACHABLE (404, private or moved); that is normal, not an error: say so in confidence.notes, set analysis_degraded true, and build the profile from the site alone. 2) Use the FireCrawl tool to scrape the live product URL (and its /pricing page if one exists). When that URL is the app itself (a login, an editor, a dashboard) and has no pricing page, the prices usually live on the brand's marketing site: follow the website or pricing link in the README, the repo's homepage field or the app's footer (e.g. name.com when the app runs on name.io) and scrape that pricing page; a pricing page missing from the app host is then not a site gap. 3) Record EVERY source you actually read in sources_read as you go, this is the builder's evidence trail for reviewing your work. 4) Synthesize both sources into the profile. Prefer evidence from the repo and site over guesses; when you must infer, mark confidence accordingly.",
          "OUTPUT CONTRACT: Respond with ONLY a single RFC 8259 JSON object, double-quoted keys and strings, no single quotes, no Python dict syntax, no markdown fences, no commentary before or after. The JSON must have exactly these keys:",
          "{\"one_liner\": string (max 140 chars, what the app does and for whom), \"description\": string (2-3 sentences), \"category\": string, \"target_user\": string (specific role/persona, not 'everyone'), \"icp\": {\"who\": string, \"pain\": string, \"current_alternatives\": [string] (NAME the actual products people use today, e.g. 'Otter.ai', not categories like 'manual note-taking', a named alternative is the seed the pricing and targeting stages search from, and a generic category gives them nothing to work with; include a manual/DIY habit only if you have already named 2+ real products), \"buying_trigger\": string}, \"differentiators\": [string] (max 4, only real ones with evidence; a compliance or certification claim (HIPAA, SOC 2, 21 CFR Part 11, GDPR, ISO 27001) names the plan or edition the source gives it, and never adds 'on all plans', 'no add-on fees' or 'out of the box' unless the source says those words), \"proof_points\": [string] (benchmarks, stars, testimonials, screenshots found, cite where each was found), \"tech_stack\": [string], \"pricing_current\": string (what pricing exists today, or 'none found'; say what usage each price buys (pageviews, seats, documents, events a month) and how each price is billed, exactly as the page shows it: a price without its usage level cannot be compared with anything, and a price labelled 'billed yearly' or 'per month, billed annually' is the yearly rate, never written as the monthly one), \"voice\": {\"tone\": [string] (3-5 adjectives describing how the builder writes, inferred from README + site copy), \"sample_phrase\": string (one representative sentence quoted from their copy)}, \"maturity\": {\"stage\": one of 'idea'|'alpha'|'beta'|'launched'|'sunset' ('sunset' when the product is acquired, archived, shut down, read-only or maintenance-only; then maturity.signals quotes the product's own notice word for word, with its URL), \"signals\": [string]}, \"gaps\": [string] (the PRODUCT's own limitations as its site, docs or repo state them: a missing platform, a feature not there yet, an early stage, a plan-only feature; each names where the product says it; EMPTY when the product states none, never a guess and never a page you could not read), \"site_gaps\": [string] (conversion essentials missing from pages you actually read: no demo link, no pricing page, no screenshots; check the page's links before listing one), \"unverified\": [string] (what you could not read or confirm: an unreachable page, a redirect, a 404, a price shown only by an animated counter, a claim without evidence; never presented as a product fact), \"confidence\": {\"overall\": number 0-1, \"notes\": string}, \"sources_read\": [{\"source\": string (the exact URL or repo file path you read, e.g. \"README.md\" or \"https://app.com/pricing\"), \"via\": \"github\"|\"firecrawl\", \"ok\": boolean (false if the fetch failed), \"note\": string (max 12 words: what it gave you, or why it failed)}], \"analysis_degraded\": boolean (true if the repo or the site could not be read at all)}",
          "RULES: Every claim in differentiators and proof_points must be traceable to something you actually read, and every source you read MUST appear in sources_read (including failed fetches, with ok=false), an empty or padded sources_read makes the profile unreviewable. If the repo or site is unreachable, still return the JSON with what you could gather, set analysis_degraded true, and explain in confidence.notes. Never invent testimonials, user counts, or benchmarks. Leave a list empty rather than filling it with guesses.",
          "HONESTY GUARDS: (1) If ANY entry in sources_read has ok=false, confidence.overall must not exceed 0.8, confidence.notes must name the failed source, and no claim may rest solely on that source, a price you could not scrape is a price you do not know. (2) Keep gaps, site_gaps and unverified apart: a read failure (a 404, a redirect, a timeout, a page that would not render) goes ONLY in unverified, never in gaps or site_gaps, because later stages publish gaps as the product's honest limitation. gaps may be empty, and empty is honest when the product states no limitation. (3) If the repository URL redirects to another repository, or its README says it is a fork, a community or self-host edition, or a distribution with features removed or not for production, record it as a gap quoting that README (e.g. the open-source edition leaves out team features and is not for production use), and never apply that repository's licence, feature set or limits to the hosted product the site sells: the open code is that edition, not the whole product, and no later stage may say the full product is open source or self-hostable."
        ],
        "max_waves": 20,
        "parameters": {}
      },
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 240,
          "y": 200
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      },
      "input": [
        {
          "lane": "questions",
          "from": "chat_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "llm_openai_api_1",
      "provider": "llm_openai_api",
      "name": "OpenAI-Compatible API",
      "config": {
        "profile": "custom",
        "custom": {
          "apikey": "${ROCKETRIDE_ANTHROPIC_KEY}",
          "base_url": "https://api.anthropic.com/v1",
          "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6",
          "modelTotalTokens": 200000
        },
        "parameters": {}
      },
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 60,
          "y": 360
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      },
      "control": [
        {
          "classType": "llm",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "memory_internal_1",
      "provider": "memory_internal",
      "name": "Memory (Internal)",
      "config": {
        "type": "memory_internal"
      },
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 240,
          "y": 360
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      },
      "control": [
        {
          "classType": "memory",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tool_github_1",
      "provider": "tool_github",
      "name": "GitHub",
      "config": {
        "type": "tool_github",
        "token": "${ROCKETRIDE_GITHUB_TOKEN}",
        "readOnly": true,
        "defaultRepo": ""
      },
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 420,
          "y": 360
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      },
      "control": [
        {
          "classType": "tool",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tool_firecrawl_1",
      "provider": "tool_firecrawl",
      "name": "Firecrawl",
      "config": {
        "type": "tool_firecrawl",
        "apikey": "${ROCKETRIDE_FIRECRAWL_KEY}"
      },
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 600,
          "y": 360
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      },
      "control": [
        {
          "classType": "tool",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "response_answers_1",
      "provider": "response_answers",
      "name": "Return Answers",
      "config": {
        "laneName": "answers"
      },
      "ui": {
        "position": {
          "x": 460,
          "y": 200
        },
        "nodeType": "default",
        "formDataValid": true
      },
      "input": [
        {
          "lane": "answers",
          "from": "agent_rocketride_1"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "project_id": "3f8066a9-3f15-4694-b1c1-0af2c6de1929",
